const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"CR_E2_001","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"In country X, while public opinion is largely in favour of private ownership or public-private partnership in the segments of air and rail transport, when it comes to road transport, the means of transport for the economically weak, the overwhelming opinion is that this should remain in the public sector. This, it is felt, is because only the government can work for social welfare on a no-profit basis.","question":"Which of the following is an assumption by the champions of public good in making a \\"road-transport in the public sector\\" demand?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Profit orientation of a service provider makes common people pay more for services.","B":"Enterprises in the public sector always run on losses.","C":"Private owners of public transport systems are generally profit-oriented.","D":"Air fares and rail transport fares are much higher than the fares for the road transport systems."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Profit orientation of a service provider makes common people pay more for services.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope","D":"irrelevant_comparison"},"explanation":"The argument concludes that road transportation is to be maintained by the government, rather than by private entities, because private entities are profit-oriented. For this conclusion to be true, we need to assume that if we look for profit, we cannot give affordable service to common people. This sense is given by Option A, which is the best option. Choice A: CORRECT as per the reasons given above. Choice B: Whether or not public sector enterprises incur losses is not related to the conclusion. Choice C: This option talks about private owners, who are not related to the conclusion. Choice D: The comparison of air fares and road transport fares is not relevant to the conclusion.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["assumption"]},{"id":"CR_E2_002","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Philanthropy works where donors are assured that their money would be put to good public use. They want to be sure that corrupt administrators and politicians would not squander away their hard-earned wealth. This comfort factor, so necessary for charity to flourish, is missing today.","question":"Which of the following conclusions can most properly be drawn from the information above?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Philanthropy functions best in societies that uphold public transparency.","B":"For individuals to give to society, society must deserve it first.","C":"Philanthropy functions best in places where public servants are trusted till they are proven guilty.","D":"Today's society does not get philanthropic donations because of the apprehensions about the misuse of funds."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Today's society does not get philanthropic donations because of the apprehensions about the misuse of funds.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"too_extreme","B":"out_of_scope","C":"too_extreme"},"explanation":"The argument posits that philanthropy works well when funds are properly utilised and that today's society seldom has that kind of transparency. Based on this we can conclude that today's society does not get much philanthropic donations because of the corrupt administrators and politicians. Choice A: The use of superlative 'best' cannot be supported by the information given in the argument. Choice B: Whether the society deserves the help or not is not within the scope of the premises. Choice C: The use of 'best' and the expression 'until proven guilty' make this option beyond the scope of the information given. Choice D: CORRECT as per the reasons given above.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["conclusion"]},{"id":"CR_E2_003","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Deepika: Now is the time to invest in a flat or a plot – real estate prices should not increase during the next six months unless interest rates drop significantly. Ankur: I disagree. Three years ago, interest rates fell significantly but real estate prices did not increase at all.","question":"It can be inferred from the above exchange that Ankur has interpreted Deepika's statement to mean that","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"interest rates are the only significant economic factor determining real estate prices.","B":"interest rates are likely to fall significantly in the next six months.","C":"real estate prices are directly proportional to interest rates.","D":"if interest rates fall, real estate prices must rise."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"if interest rates fall, real estate prices must rise.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"confuses_inference_with_assumption","B":"out_of_scope","C":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"In response to Deepika's argument that real estate prices won't rise unless interest rates drop, Ankur gives a counter-example of when rates did fall but prices didn't rise. This implies Ankur interprets Deepika as meaning that falling interest rates must always lead to rising real estate prices. Choice A: This cannot be the interpretation, as it argues against Ankur's own statement. Choice B: Ankur's interpretation is not about the future likelihood of interest rate movement. Choice C: The relationship is actually inverse — if interest rates come down, real estate prices go up — so 'directly proportional' is incorrect. Choice D: CORRECT as per the reasons given above.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["inference"]},{"id":"CR_E2_004","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"The fact that the pictures of Taj Mahal are found even on chocolate-boxes seems to reduce the aesthetic value of the Monument. Many tourist guide books seem to entreat us to read Mughal Emperor Shah Jahan's mausoleum as the world's greatest monument of love. Like those of Mona Lisa, the imitations of Taj Mahal certainly downgrade value of the Monument.","question":"Which of the following, if true, would most seriously undermine the validity of the above conclusion?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"When you arrive in Agra every hawker peddles imitations of Taj of every size and price to make the familiarity-breeds-contempt problem worse.","B":"Recently, a British friend making his first trip to India decided to leave the Taj off his itinerary because of its over-exposure.","C":"A piece of art is imitated and branded most because of its insurmountable beauty.","D":"One's imagination of Taj is tarnished by cruel tales of the master mason's hands being cut off so he would never build anything lovelier."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"A piece of art is imitated and branded most because of its insurmountable beauty.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The argument concludes that freely available imitations of Taj Mahal reduce its aesthetic value. We need the option that weakens this conclusion. Choice A: This actually strengthens the argument — more imitations make the familiarity-breeds-contempt problem worse. Choice B: This also strengthens the argument, as over-exposure deters visitors. Choice C: CORRECT — if imitations proliferate because of a work's insurmountable beauty, then the imitations actually prove the beauty of the Taj rather than diminishing it, directly undermining the conclusion. Choice D: This is not related to the imitations and their effect on the aesthetic value of the Taj.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["weaken"]},{"id":"CR_E2_005","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Social networking is a love-hate relationship. On the one hand, managers want their workers to experiment so they can cultivate new-world skills. Employees as brand ambassadors! Products virtually transformed into overnight hits! On the other hand, bosses are filled with foreboding about social networking's dark side – losing secrets to rivals, the corporate embarrassment of errant employee tweets, becoming the latest victim of a venomous crowd.","question":"Which of the following conclusions can most properly be drawn from the information above?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Social networking is fast becoming the way people and companies collaborate and find new business.","B":"Employers must discipline and fire workers for their internet airings.","C":"Employees must stay away from controversial topics that aren't related to their corporate role.","D":"Companies are scrambling to silence errant messages while exploiting social networks."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Companies are scrambling to silence errant messages while exploiting social networks.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"too_extreme","C":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"The argument talks about both positive and negative aspects of social networking from a corporate perspective. We need a conclusion that captures both sides. Choice A: This does not include the negative aspects given at the end of the argument. Choice B: This talks only about the negative aspect and is beyond the scope of the argument by talking about firing. Choice C: This neglects the positive aspect of social networking. Choice D: CORRECT — this option includes both the negative aspect (silencing errant messages) and the positive aspect (exploiting social networks) mentioned in the argument.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["conclusion"]},{"id":"CR_E2_006","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"The level of industrial pollutants in the water bodies of a state has fallen by an average of 20% during the last five years. The government claims the credit for the decrease by mentioning the Clean Water Act passed by it five years ago. The opposition, however, attributes the decrease to the reduced industrial activity due to the recession because of which at least 10% of the businesses closed during the period.","question":"Which of the following if true, would most seriously weaken the opposition's argument?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Few corporations migrated to other states during the past five years to avoid the stringent regulations of the Clean Water Act.","B":"The officials charged with the enforcement of the Act have prosecuted only two violators of the law, due to budgetary constraints.","C":"All but 5% of industries that shut down in the state during the recession were treating their waste before discharging it into water bodies.","D":"Economic conditions in the country during the past five years have been worse than those within the state."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"All but 5% of industries that shut down in the state during the recession were treating their waste before discharging it into water bodies.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The opposition argues that reduced industrial activity due to recession is the reason for reduced pollutants in water bodies. We need to weaken this claim. Choice A: The fact that few corporations moved out does not weaken the opposition's conclusion about recession being the cause. Choice B: Information about enforcement is not relevant to the opposition's claim about recession. Choice C: CORRECT — if the industries that shut down during the recession were already treating their waste before discharge, their closure would not have reduced pollutants. This removes the causal link between recession-related closures and reduced pollution. Choice D: Broader economic conditions are irrelevant to the decrease in pollutants within the state.","time_benchmark_seconds":105,"question_type":["weaken"]},{"id":"CR_E2_007","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Households in the city recently started the segregation of waste into biodegradable (mostly food and organic matter like fruit and vegetable peels) and non-biodegradable (like plastic covers, cartons etc). A study of the garbage of a large number of average-sized households has found that a household discards more non-biodegradable waste when its food is made up of more canned and pre-cooked packaged foods. Such households discard less biodegradable waste.","question":"It can be inferred from the above that:","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"the segregation of garbage makes families more conscious of the kind of food they consume.","B":"an increasing number of urban households rely on canned and packaged foods.","C":"the more fresh produce a household buys, the healthier it is.","D":"the more fresh produce a household consumes, the more biodegradable waste it produces."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"the more fresh produce a household consumes, the more biodegradable waste it produces.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"confuses_inference_with_assumption","B":"correct_but_unstated","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The argument establishes that higher use of canned food leads to more non-biodegradable waste and less biodegradable waste — and vice versa. We need a direct inference. Choice A: The segregation may or may not result in consciousness about food — this cannot be inferred. Choice B: Based on the information, we cannot infer that the number of canned food eaters is increasing. Choice C: The health of a person cannot be inferred from the information given. Choice D: CORRECT — this must be true as the inverse of the stated relationship: less canned food means more fresh produce and therefore more biodegradable waste.","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_type":["inference"]},{"id":"CR_E2_008","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"X: Listening to your inner voice is not always a good thing. The voice can be irritating and even negative with its constant warnings, it can get you down. But not listening to it is difficult. Trying to suppress the voices can make them louder and more insistent. Y: But you should not just listen to one voice. If the devil is on your left shoulder, the angel will be on your right and they are whispering in your ears – you must listen to both. Bring your inner voices together and listen carefully to what they have to say.","question":"Which of the following is the conclusion intended by Y?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The voices surface only when a person has been psychologically battered.","B":"Inner voices have something meaningful to say.","C":"People who only see the bad often have to struggle to learn to look in all directions again.","D":"A person cannot prevent negative thoughts."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Inner voices have something meaningful to say.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","C":"correct_but_unstated","D":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"X argues we should not always listen to inner voices, while Y argues that we should listen to both good and bad inner voices and bring them together to arrive at a judgment. Choice A: Y's argument is not about the psychologically battered state — this is out of scope. Choice B: CORRECT — Y's overall point is that both inner voices (positive and negative) have something meaningful to contribute and should be listened to together. Choice C: Y argues that we should bring both bad and good voices together; the sense of struggling to 'look in all directions' is not given by Y's argument. Choice D: Y has concluded about both good and bad thoughts, not just about bad thoughts, so this is incomplete.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["conclusion"]},{"id":"CR_E2_009","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"The number of patients who died after they were admitted and treated at 'Citicare' hospital is more than that at 'Medicare' hospital. This clearly shows that the medical treatment at 'Medicare' is better than that at 'Citicare' and a patient should seek treatment at 'Medicare' hospital.","question":"Which of the following, if true, casts the most serious doubt on the conclusion above?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"At any time, 'Medicare' has more patients taking treatment than 'Citicare.'","B":"The treatment charges at 'Medicare' are higher compared to those at 'Citicare'.","C":"The diagnostic equipment available at 'Citicare' is superior to those at 'Medicare'.","D":"While 'Medicare' usually treats patients in the early stages of their disease, 'Citicare' treats patients in the most advanced stage of their diseases."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"While 'Medicare' usually treats patients in the early stages of their disease, 'Citicare' treats patients in the most advanced stage of their diseases.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"irrelevant_comparison","B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The argument concludes that Medicare's treatment is better based on higher death numbers at Citicare. We need to weaken this. Choice A: The popularity or size of a hospital does not necessarily reflect the quality of treatment. Choice B: Treatment charges do not decisively prove that quality of treatment is better. Choice C: Quality of diagnostic equipment is not a necessary reflection of treatment quality. Choice D: CORRECT — if Citicare treats patients in the most advanced stages of disease, higher deaths there do not indicate inferior treatment but rather reflect the severity of cases admitted. This directly undermines the conclusion.","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_type":["weaken"]},{"id":"CR_E2_010","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Jim: I underwent medical treatment under Doctor X for thirty days for my backache. There was absolutely no improvement. After this I went to Doctor Y for treatment. We are into the third week of treatment and already the condition of my back has improved vastly. Both the doctors follow the same system of medicine though their ways of treatment are different. Hence Doctor Y's treatment is definitely more effective than Doctor X's treatment.","question":"Which of the following, if true, repudiates Jim's claims?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Doctor X's treatment usually takes about 40 days to show results.","B":"Doctor X treats a wide variety of body pains whereas Doctor Y concentrates only on back pains.","C":"Doctor X started his practice much earlier than Doctor Y.","D":"While Doctor X is easily accessible, Doctor Y can be approached only after seeking an appointment 15 days in advance."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Doctor X's treatment usually takes about 40 days to show results.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"B":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Jim claims Doctor Y's treatment is better because Doctor X showed no improvement in 30 days while Doctor Y showed improvement in 3 weeks. We need to weaken this claim. Choice A: CORRECT — if Doctor X's treatment typically takes about 40 days to show results, Jim left before the treatment could work. The subsequent improvement under Doctor Y may actually be the delayed result of Doctor X's treatment, directly repudiating Jim's claim. Choice B: If Doctor Y is a specialist, this would actually strengthen Jim's claim that Doctor Y's treatment is better. Choice C: Length of experience is not necessarily related to better treatment. Choice D: Accessibility of the doctor does not relate to the quality of treatment.","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_type":["weaken"]},{"id":"CR_E2_011","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"The literacy rate of a small island, 'Emerald' with a population of about a million was found to be nearly 60% in a survey conducted two years back. A massive earthquake last year claimed the lives of almost one lakh people in the island. A survey taken this year shows that the literacy rate has gone up to 64% in the island. The number of births and deaths (due to other reasons) were not significant in the island in the two year period. The government claims that it was due to its efforts that the literacy rate has gone up in the island.","question":"Which of the following, if true, most seriously weakens the government's claim?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Almost all the people who lost their lives in the earthquake belonged to a city that had 100% literacy.","B":"The total population in the island has come down since the last survey.","C":"Literacy rates will always go up because an illiterate can become a literate but the converse case is not possible.","D":"Among the victims of the earthquakes, a vast majority were illiterates."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Among the victims of the earthquakes, a vast majority were illiterates.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","B":"correct_but_unstated","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The government claims its efforts caused the literacy rate to increase. We need to weaken this — ideally by providing an alternative explanation. If the earthquake killed mostly illiterate people, the literacy rate would rise mechanically, with no credit to the government. Choice A: If those who died had 100% literacy, it would actually strengthen the government's claim since literacy went up despite losing literate people. Choice B: Without knowing whether the decrease is in literates or illiterates, we cannot determine if this strengthens or weakens the claim. Choice C: This information is irrelevant to the causal relationship between government efforts and the increase in literacy. Choice D: CORRECT — if the vast majority of earthquake victims were illiterates, the literacy rate would rise simply because illiterates were removed from the population, not because of any government effort.","time_benchmark_seconds":105,"question_type":["weaken"]},{"id":"CR_E2_012","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"In a recent survey conducted in a city, it was found that among three hundred houses which had burglar alarms, a burglary attempt was made only in one house, during the last three years. This clearly proves that fitting houses with burglar alarms is an effective deterrent to burglary.","question":"Which among the following, if true, exposes the most serious flaw in the above conclusion?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"In the houses which had manual security during the day, the burglar alarms were activated only during nights.","B":"The burglar alarms were fitted in such a manner that their presence was not noticeable to outsiders.","C":"The burglaries were committed usually only during nights.","D":"The three-year survey period saw a spurt in robberies and attempted robberies in the city."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"The burglar alarms were fitted in such a manner that their presence was not noticeable to outsiders.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","C":"out_of_scope","D":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption"},"explanation":"The argument concludes that burglar alarms deter burglary. We need to identify a factor that proves the alarms are not the actual reason for low burglary. Choice A: If alarms are active during night (when burglaries mostly occur), this actually strengthens the argument. Choice B: CORRECT — if the alarms were not visible to outsiders, burglars would not know to avoid those houses because of the alarms. Low burglary attempts cannot then be attributed to the deterrent effect of alarms, exposing a serious flaw in the conclusion. Choice C: The timing of burglaries does not relate to whether alarms caused the deterrence. Choice D: If there was a general spurt in burglaries across the city but few in alarm-fitted houses, this actually strengthens the argument that alarms are an effective deterrent.","time_benchmark_seconds":105,"question_type":["flaw"]},{"id":"CR_E2_013","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Oceanica has ordered withdrawal of its troops from 'Nationalina', a country which was under its occupation for the past three years. Earlier this year, 'Allina' a country friendly to 'Nationalina' had sent its troops to 'Nationalina' to fight along with its army. 'Allina' claims that only its action of sending troops to 'Nationalina' forced 'Oceanica' to withdraw its troops from 'Nationalina'.","question":"Which of the following, if true, casts the most serious doubt on the claims of 'Allina'?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The number of Nationalina troops was small compared to that of Oceanica's army.","B":"Oceanica had never thought of leaving Nationalina till about a year ago.","C":"A new government which had come to power in Oceanica this year had in principle decided to withdraw from all occupied territories.","D":"There was no appeal from 'Nationalina' to 'Oceanica' to vacate its occupied territory."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"A new government which had come to power in Oceanica this year had in principle decided to withdraw from all occupied territories.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"premise_restatement","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Allina claims its military action caused Oceanica to withdraw. We need to find an alternative explanation for the withdrawal. Choice A: Information about the size of Nationalina's troops does not give information about Allina's army and is irrelevant to disproving the causal connection. Choice B: This information actually strengthens Allina's claim by suggesting Oceanica's decision to leave coincided with Allina's involvement. Choice C: CORRECT — if a new government in Oceanica had already decided to withdraw from all occupied territories, Oceanica's withdrawal from Nationalina is explained by its own foreign policy change, not by Allina's military action. Choice D: The absence of an appeal from Nationalina does not weaken the causal claim made by Allina.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["weaken"]},{"id":"CR_E2_014","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Scientists studying the formation of ice on oceans have found that 'ice age' has been cyclic and has invariably occurred once in five hundred years ever since known history of mankind. This means that the next ice age is due to occur in this century itself. So, all predictions regarding melting of ocean ice due to global warming are unwarranted, according to these scientists.","question":"Which of the following, if true, casts the most serious doubt on the claim of some scientists that predictions of global warming are unfounded?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Ice ages also have harmful consequences though not as severe as the effects of global warming.","B":"Ice ages were found to occur quickly in the years preceding the known origin of humankind on earth.","C":"Ice ages do not last for more than ten years.","D":"Last century saw a large-scale use of motor vehicles and refrigerators whose effect on ice melting in oceans is much more than all the factors that prevented ice formation in the earlier years."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Last century saw a large-scale use of motor vehicles and refrigerators whose effect on ice melting in oceans is much more than all the factors that prevented ice formation in the earlier years.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The scientists claim that global warming fears are unfounded because an ice age is due soon. We need to prove that global warming fears are in fact justified. Choice A: Information about harmful consequences of ice ages does not prove that global warming is harmful. Choice B: The frequency of ice ages before humankind is not relevant to their occurrence in the present century. Choice C: How long ice ages last is not related to whether global warming fears are valid. Choice D: CORRECT — if modern industrial activity has created ice-melting forces far greater than any natural ice-forming factors of the past, this suggests global warming could overpower the expected ice age cycle, justifying fears about ice melting.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["weaken"]},{"id":"CR_E2_015","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"30 students of a class were tested on archery. These students were then given a seven-day training course in archery. When the students were tested again after the course, they showed a forty percent improvement in accuracy. Thus, it is clear that the course is helpful in improving accuracy in archery.","question":"Which of the following, if true, weakens the argument above?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"A three-day short-term course conducted by the same tutors did not yield proportionate increase in efficiency.","B":"The thirty students also practised archery daily for two hours apart from fulfilling the requirements of the course.","C":"In an archery competition held just after the course, where students from other schools participated as well, the students of this referred school could not win any prize.","D":"Similar training courses conducted for other sports did not improve the efficiency of the participants."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"The thirty students also practised archery daily for two hours apart from fulfilling the requirements of the course.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The argument concludes that the seven-day training course is the reason for the improvement in accuracy. We need an alternative explanation for the improvement. Choice A: This proves the three-day course is not as good as the seven-day course, which actually implies the seven-day course is useful — strengthening the argument. Choice B: CORRECT — if the students also practised independently for two hours daily, the improvement could be attributed to the additional practice rather than the course itself, directly weakening the conclusion. Choice C: Not winning a competition does not mean the training was a failure — this is not relevant to the conclusion. Choice D: If a similar program fails in other sports, it does not prove that this program fails in archery.","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_type":["weaken"]},{"id":"CR_E2_016","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Global warming has resulted in the melting of ice in oceans leading to eventual evaporation of potable water from the earth's surface. Thus, global warming is solely responsible for the significant depletion of drinking water resources in the globe by about 20 percent in the last 10 years.","question":"Which of the following, if true, weakens the argument that global warming is solely responsible for the depletion of water resources on earth?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"There have been a few years in the last decade when the global temperature actually showed a decrease.","B":"Global warming results in other harmful consequences such as loss of marine organisms.","C":"Water from other water bodies also tend to evaporate.","D":"Human population and animal population on earth have registered a significant rise in the last 10 years."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Human population and animal population on earth have registered a significant rise in the last 10 years.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption","B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The argument claims global warming is solely responsible for the 20% depletion of drinking water. We need to identify another significant cause. Choice A: A few years of temperature decrease actually strengthens the argument — if temperatures still mostly rose, global warming remains the likely sole cause. Choice B: Other harmful consequences of global warming are not about drinking water shortage. Choice C: The possibility of other water bodies evaporating is not directly relevant to the conclusion about drinking water depletion. Choice D: CORRECT — a significant rise in human and animal population directly increases consumption of drinking water, providing a more cogent alternative reason for the depletion of drinking water resources beyond global warming alone.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["weaken"]},{"id":"CR_E2_017","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Some popular energy drinks available in the market can provide 100 percent of the recommended daily requirement of vitamins. Nevertheless, a well-balanced meal, including a variety of foods, is a better source of those vitamins than are such fortified energy drinks alone.","question":"Which of the following, if true, would most strongly support the position above?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Since buying one bottle of such drinks requires less effort than planning and selecting food items to make a balanced diet, people prefer the former.","B":"People may not get their daily requirement of vitamins from foods since they eat insufficient fruits and vegetables.","C":"The combination of vitamins with other nutrients in food makes those vitamins more usable by the body than vitamins added to energy drinks.","D":"Natural food products are easier to digest than fortified energy drinks."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"The combination of vitamins with other nutrients in food makes those vitamins more usable by the body than vitamins added to energy drinks.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"The argument concludes that a well-balanced meal is a better source of vitamins than energy drinks. We need to find a factor that supports this view. Choice A: People's preference for energy drinks due to convenience actually supports the use of energy drinks, not the argument. Choice B: People not getting sufficient vitamins from food actually supports the case for energy drinks. Choice C: CORRECT — if the combination of vitamins with other nutrients in whole food makes those vitamins more bioavailable than vitamins added artificially to energy drinks, this directly proves that a well-balanced meal is a better source. Choice D: Easier digestion does not address the specific claim about vitamins being a better source.","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_type":["strengthen"]},{"id":"CR_E2_018","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Rohan and Lila earn the same wages per hour for working in the same company. However, Rohan's job is more strenuous than that of Lila. Rohan, therefore, argues that because his job is more demanding, he deserves a better pay than Lila.","question":"Which of the following statements best supports Rohan's line of reasoning?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Rohan has more years of experience than Lila has.","B":"The company has never unequivocally taken a stand that women should be paid at the same rate as men.","C":"Rohan is in any case due for a promotion.","D":"The salary structure of the company is designed to give greater compensation for a job that is physically more demanding."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The salary structure of the company is designed to give greater compensation for a job that is physically more demanding.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Rohan argues that his more strenuous and demanding job deserves better pay. We need information that directly supports this line of reasoning. Choice A: Experience in a job does not support the view that more strenuous work deserves more salary — it is not relevant to his argument. Choice B: The company's silence about gender equality does not support Rohan's argument about pay for strenuousness. Choice C: A due promotion does not support his argument about differential pay for demanding work. Choice D: CORRECT — if the company's own salary structure is designed to compensate more for physically demanding jobs, Rohan's argument for higher pay is directly and formally justified.","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_type":["strengthen"]},{"id":"CR_E2_019","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Mike Briffin, the leader of the BNP (a party with 'whites only' policy), has been catapulted into the European Parliament. It's tempting to dismiss those who vote and stand for BNP as stupid, but the truth is more disturbing. Briffin is an apparently intelligent Cambridge graduate. Yet he is unable to think himself out of a racist framework. Many of us would like to believe that intellect banishes prejudice but it is not necessarily so.","question":"All of the sentiments expressed below by great philosophers strengthen the above conclusion EXCEPT","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"David Humes in his footnote to the essay 'Of National characters' said 'I'm apt to suspect the negroes to be naturally inferior to the whites'.","B":"Another Enlightenment icon, Immanuel Kant, in his 'Observations on the feeling of the Beautiful and the Sublime' said 'The blacks are very vain but in the Negroes' way, and so talkative that they must be driven apart with thrashing'.","C":"Samuel Johnson was no cosmopolitan and nurtured a prejudice against the Scots.","D":"Johnson quipped in a pamphlet about America: 'How is it that we hear the loudest yelps for liberty among the drivers of negroes?'"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Johnson quipped in a pamphlet about America: 'How is it that we hear the loudest yelps for liberty among the drivers of negroes?'","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","C":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"The argument concludes that even intelligent people can harbour prejudice. Options A, B, and C all show educated philosophers expressing racial prejudice, which strengthens this conclusion. We need to identify the EXCEPT — the one that does not strengthen it. Choice A: Hume's statement implies racial inferiority — this substantiates the general conclusion that even intellectuals hold prejudice. Choice B: Kant's statement expresses racial prejudice — this also substantiates the conclusion. Choice C: Johnson's prejudice against the Scots also proves the existence of prejudice in an intellectual. Choice D: CORRECT (EXCEPT) — Johnson's rhetorical question actually challenges the rationality of those who fight for liberty while practising slavery. It is a critique of prejudice, not an expression of it, and therefore does not strengthen the argument that intellect fails to banish prejudice.","time_benchmark_seconds":105,"question_type":["strengthen_except"]},{"id":"CR_E2_020","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Restaurants and hotels are badly mismanaged. Fifty years ago, a satisfying meal used to cost less than a dollar. Since then the prices have increased at least tenfold with an actual decrease in quality and taste.","question":"Which of the following statements, if true, would tend to strengthen the argument above?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The number of people eating out has increased dramatically over the last fifty years.","B":"Restaurants and hotels today focus more on ambience and appearance than on the food served.","C":"The quality of home food is always thought to be superior to what can be had at restaurants and hotels.","D":"The number of hotels and restaurants is several times what it was fifty years ago."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Restaurants and hotels today focus more on ambience and appearance than on the food served.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The argument concludes that restaurants charge more but deliver lower quality food. We need a factor that explains both higher prices and lower food quality. Choice A: Increased number of people eating out is not related to the quality or pricing of the food. Choice B: CORRECT — if restaurants today focus more on ambience and appearance than on food, they spend money on these aspects rather than on food preparation, which explains both higher prices and lower food quality — directly strengthening the argument. Choice C: The quality of home food does not decide the quality of food served in a restaurant. Choice D: An increase in the number of hotels and restaurants does not explain the decreasing food quality over the period.","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_type":["strengthen"]},{"id":"CR_E2_021","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Amniocentesis is a test designed to detect chromosomal abnormality in the unborn foetus. In India it is used to determine the sex of the foetus to help parents to abort the female they do not desire. Though the test has been banned by law, both those seeking the test and those conducting it are culpable, it is clear that in India it is very much used for the undesirable purpose.","question":"Which of the following strengthens the above conclusion?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"All hospitals, diagnostic centers and doctors' clinics have posters informing people that amniocentesis is illegal.","B":"The test has shifted from being an overt to a covert practice.","C":"Many religious, traditional and cultural beliefs reinforce the importance of sons over daughters.","D":"The national demographic census shows that the male to female ratio of new born which stood at 917 to 1000, a natural proportion worldwide, has been reversed in India."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The national demographic census shows that the male to female ratio of new born which stood at 917 to 1000, a natural proportion worldwide, has been reversed in India.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The argument concludes that amniocentesis is being illegally used for sex determination in India. We need a factor that supports this view. Choice A: Hospitals having posters about illegality shows awareness of the ban but does not prove that the test is being used illegally. Choice B: The test being covert rather than overt does not prove it is being used for sex determination specifically. Choice C: Cultural beliefs favouring sons reinforce the motive but do not directly prove the test is being used for sex determination. Choice D: CORRECT — the census data showing that the natural male-to-female birth ratio has been reversed in India provides factual and statistical proof that female foetuses are being selectively aborted, which can only be accomplished through sex determination tests like amniocentesis.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["strengthen"]},{"id":"CR_E2_022","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"The recent attack of flu is far more severe than such attacks usually are and take longer to cure. Doctors believe the cause of the flu to be a certain bacterium. However, recently released data mentions that the bacterium seems to thrive in the presence of a certain virus. Some doctors are of the opinion that it is the virus that actually causes the flu.","question":"Which of the following would most strengthen the opinion of some doctors?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Onset of the flu usually follows infection by both the virus and the bacterium.","B":"The bacterium is seen to grow and multiply better in the presence of the virus, when the flu attack begins.","C":"The flu has been observed to follow infection by the bacterium even in the absence of the virus.","D":"In many instances of the flu attack, the virus alone has been detected."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"In many instances of the flu attack, the virus alone has been detected.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption","C":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"Some doctors claim that the virus, not the bacterium, causes the flu. We need to strengthen this view. Choice A: Flu following infection by both the virus and bacterium does not clearly establish whether it is the virus or the bacteria that causes the infection. Choice B: The proliferation of bacteria in the presence of the virus supports the bacterial genesis of flu — this weakens the doctors' claim about the virus. Choice C: Flu following bacterial infection even in the absence of the virus decisively proves that bacteria is the reason — this directly weakens the claim. Choice D: CORRECT — if flu attacks are found with the virus alone (and no bacterium), this finding proves that the virus alone is sufficient to cause the flu, strongly supporting the claim that the virus is responsible.","time_benchmark_seconds":105,"question_type":["strengthen"]},{"id":"CR_E2_023","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Jacob: It is unfair on the part of parents to compel their teenaged children to accept their choices on any issue. Since parents desire freedom of choice and detest being forced by others to accept anything, they should also not expect their teenaged children to follow their suggestions.","question":"Which of the following, if true, can strengthen an argument against the one used by Jacob?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Children these days are much more informed than parents and so can take independent decisions.","B":"In human lives, experience has always played a significant part in making the right choices.","C":"Children, who are constantly monitored by parents, become incapable of taking decisions in their later lives.","D":"Present day parents might not have obeyed their parents when they were children."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"In human lives, experience has always played a significant part in making the right choices.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption","C":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Jacob argues against parents compelling teenagers. The question asks us to find information that strengthens an argument against Jacob — i.e., justifies parents' compelling. Choice A: If teenagers are better informed, it is not wise to force them — this actually weakens the case against Jacob. Choice B: CORRECT — if experience plays a significant part in making the right choices, parents with their greater experience have the ability to guide teenagers better, justifying their compelling and strengthening the argument against Jacob. Choice C: If constant monitoring makes children incapable, parents should not force their children — this also weakens the case against Jacob. Choice D: Whether present parents obeyed their own parents in the past is not relevant to whether current teenagers should obey their parents.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["strengthen"]},{"id":"CR_E2_024","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Images on the television screen move in rapid succession whereas a book can be read at the reader's pace. Further, information in a book can be visited any number of times. Thus, educational programmes for children through television will not be as successful as teaching with the aid of books.","question":"Which of the following, if true, casts the most serious doubt on the above argument?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"While viewing TV may impair the eyes of a child, reading books will not.","B":"With undiverted attention at what they see and a fertile mind, a child needs but a quick glance to grasp an information.","C":"Children are more fond of TV programmes than elders.","D":"From time immemorial children have been imparted knowledge through stories, as they like them."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"With undiverted attention at what they see and a fertile mind, a child needs but a quick glance to grasp an information.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The argument concludes that television is less effective than books for educating children, partly because TV images move rapidly. We need a factor that supports the use of television. Choice A: This brings out a negative aspect of television, actually strengthening the argument for books. Choice B: CORRECT — if children, with their focused attention and receptive minds, can grasp information from a single quick glance at television, the argument that rapid succession of images is a drawback is directly undermined. This supports the view that television can be equally or more effective. Choice C: Children's fondness for TV programmes over adults' is not relevant to the conclusion about educational effectiveness. Choice D: Imparting knowledge through stories is not relevant to comparing TV versus books as educational tools.","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_type":["weaken"]},{"id":"CR_E2_025","exercise":"exercise_2","passage":"Increasing demand for a number of goods drives up their prices. If tax rates are enhanced, it will reduce the quantum of money in the hands of the spending public. Thus, enhanced tax rates will result in control of prices of goods.","question":"Which of the following, if true, most seriously undermines the above argument?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Increasing tax rates will make the government unpopular with the public, a situation that no government would want.","B":"Reduced demand may lead to fall in production and hence reduce the real income of the public.","C":"Non-wage earners cannot be subjected to increase in tax rates and hence the move will not be effective.","D":"Any reduction of money in their hands usually drives people to cut their investments to amounts equal to such reduction."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Any reduction of money in their hands usually drives people to cut their investments to amounts equal to such reduction.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Critical Reasoning","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The argument posits that increased tax rates reduce money in public hands, which reduces demand and thereby controls prices. We need to prove that demand does not actually go down. Choice A: Government popularity is not related to the effect of increased taxes on demand for goods. Choice B: This talks about indirect effects of reduced demand — it does not show that demand will actually fail to decrease. Choice C: Information about non-wage earners is not relevant to the effect of increased taxes on wage earners' demand; the argument also does not explicitly tie taxes to wage earners only. Choice D: CORRECT — if people respond to reduced money in their hands by cutting investments (not spending), their expenditure on goods remains the same. Demand therefore does not decrease, prices do not fall, and the argument's chain of reasoning is directly broken.","time_benchmark_seconds":105,"question_type":["weaken"]}]`);export{e as default};
